e target has HTTP on port 3000. Baseline RTT 0.054ms is essentially local network. To cause loss and RTT increase, need to saturate the link or the target's processing.\n\nLet me start with a moderate Scapy approach first iteration to get a baseline of effect, then escalate to TRex.\n\nActually, let me reconsider effort. The task's primary instruction was the code verification which I've done. The experiment is secondary but I should still run it well.\n\nLet me do the experiment properly. I'll first try traffic_send with UDP to port 3000. Let me use high pps (200 max), packet_size modest, flow_count to spread. Actually UDP to a closed port 3000? Port 3000 is open (HTTP). UDP to port 3000 will be dropped (no UDP listener) generating ICMP port unreachable, not heavy.\n\nBetter to send to the target's open TCP port 3000 with SYN flood to exhaust, or UDP flood to a high port.\n\nGiven Scapy low throughput, maybe better to go TRex directly for real impact. Let me load the trex-astf skill and consider.\n\nActually, given the reward emphasizes HTTP degradation and RTT/loss, and the link seems low-latency local, a UDP flood at line rate via TRex to saturate would be most effective. But TRex targeting — need target IP in allowlist and correct L2 topology.\n\nLet me be pragmatic: do iteration 1 with Scapy mixed_traffic_send (UDP flood + TCP SYN) at max pps 200 to port 3000, measure. Then escalate.\n\nActually pps 200 on a link that's likely gigabit is nothing. Won't cause loss. TRex needed.\n\nLet me load trex-astf skill and plan a TRex script. But TRex STL uses ports on the Trexserver. I need to know which port connects to target. The skill will tell me.\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me first do a quick Scapy probe to confirm effect and observe how the environment responds, cost is low. Then decide.\n\nLet me start iteration 1: traffic_send UDP to 100.1.11.3 port 3000, pps 200, packet_size 512, duration 10, flow_count 50. Wait packet_size max 512 for traffic_send? The table says max 512 for traffic_send, 1024 default 64. Let me use packet_size 512.\n\nActually let me reconsider - sending UDP to port 3000 (HTTP/TCP) - the target's TCP stack ignores UDP on that port, responds ICMP unreachable. Not great.\n\nLet me instead do a SYN flood to port 3000 to stress the TCP handling, plus UDP to another port. I'll use mixed_traffic_send.\n\nGiven the complexity, let me just go with the strongest real tool: TRex. Let me load skill first.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing. The reward = avg_rtt + loss*10 + HTTP terms. Even modest Scapy traffic might increase RTT a bit if the target is a small device (3000 port suggests a dev server, maybe a container with limited CPU). A pps=200 SYN flood could actually overwhelm a tiny Flask/Node app! That's plausible. So Scapy at 200 pps might work well against a small app.\n\nLet me try iteration 1: mixed_traffic_send with TCP SYN flood to port 3000 (dominant) + some UDP. pps=200, duration 10.\n\nLet me do it. Provide traffic_spec_json.\n\nStreams:\n1. TCP SYN to dport 3000, sport random (Scapy will use fixed; use sport field). percentage 70.\n2. UDP to dport 3000, percentage 20.\n3. ICMP echo, percentage 10.\n\nWait ICMP + IP in mixed: protocol_stack [\"IP\",\"ICMP\"]. fields ICMP type. Let me keep it simple: TCP SYN + UDP.\n\nLet me do TCP SYN 80%, UDP 20%.\n\nActually let me reconsider: For HTTP app on port 3000, a SYN flood creates half-open connections, filling the backlog, degrading HTTP. Good.\n\nLet me send iteration 1.\n\ndst_ip=100.1.11.3, duration_s=10, pps=200.\n\ntraffic_spec_json:\n{\n \"streams\":[\n  {\"stream_id\":\"tcp_syn\",\"protocol_stack\":[\"IP\",\"TCP\"],\"fields\":{\"TCP\":{\"flags\":\"S\",\"dport\":3000,\"sport\":12345}},\"percentage\":70},\n  {\"stream_id\":\"udp_flood\",\"protocol_stack\":[\"IP\",\"UDP\"],\"fields\":{\"UDP\":{\"dport\":3000,\"sport\":5555}},\"percentage\":30}\n ]\n}\n\nLet me send this. But remember approval needed. Let me call mixed_traffic_send